Well...  Crossword puzzles are popular in Finland.  The standard Finnish
words end in a vowel (eight in total) or one of the following
consonants: t, s, n, l, r (the last two being rare).  Obviously, Finnish
crossword puzzles have foreign-language names and recent loans in them
to allow a greater selection.  But you can rule out many possible
combinations because of the structure of the Finnish language (eg. that
certain vowels can't appear with certain other vowels in non-compound
words or recent loans and that plurals always end in t).

My point seems to be that despite the constraints of a language it is
possible to make interesting crossword puzzles.  Granted, the
constraints of Italian are more severe than in Finnish -- but there are
much more consonant clusters.

(The native or native-like words include only the following letters in
Finnish: aeiouyäö + dhjklmnprstv and g only in the combination "ng";
recent loans have also b, f and g.  Not going into Finnish phonotactics
more deeply. :>) 

